WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
Autonomous discovery of human-aligned facial expressions on social robots using reinforcement learning.

00:06.000 --> 00:12.000
On many robot platforms, expressions are still manually authored as fixed presets.

00:12.000 --> 00:18.000
Designers tune parameters by hand, usually ending with only a small set of rigid expressions.

00:18.000 --> 00:22.000
We replace fixed preset design with automatic expression discovery.

00:22.000 --> 00:26.000
Our system explores the robot's facial controls and produces expression pools.

00:26.000 --> 00:32.000
Multiple candidate expressions for the same emotion, instead of one handcrafted face.

00:32.000 --> 00:37.000
We begin with four different Furhat characters, each starting from a neutral face.

00:37.000 --> 00:44.000
The goal is not to manually design one expression, but to let the system discover expressive alternatives for each character.

00:44.000 --> 00:47.000
The agent explores how each face can change.

00:47.000 --> 00:55.000
Instead of relying on fixed handcrafted presets, it searches through the robot's facial controls to discover expressions that better match a target emotion.

00:56.000 --> 01:01.000
Each explored configuration is rendered on Furhat as a visible facial expression.

01:01.000 --> 01:05.000
This gives the system a concrete candidate face that can be evaluated.

01:05.000 --> 01:10.000
The rendered face is then passed to an emotion recognizer trained on human facial expressions.

01:10.000 --> 01:14.000
It estimates which emotion the face most strongly conveys.

01:14.000 --> 01:19.000
Clearer expressions receive higher scores, while ambiguous faces are discouraged.

01:19.000 --> 01:24.000
Over many trials, this feedback guides the system toward more recognizable robot expressions.

01:24.000 --> 01:27.000
After training, the result is not a single fixed preset.

01:27.000 --> 01:36.000
For each character, the system discovers a pool of candidate expressions, giving multiple ways to express the same emotion with different visual styles and intensities.

01:36.000 --> 01:42.000
This is Vinnie's best fear episode, shown from the neutral starting face to the best scoring step.

01:42.000 --> 01:50.000
The progression shows how the expression develops gradually during training, ending at the strongest fear configuration found in that episode.

01:51.000 --> 01:54.000
This second example shows Maurice's best anger episode.

01:54.000 --> 02:02.000
The video begins at the neutral face and ends at the best scoring angry expression, showing the path the system followed before reaching its strongest result.

02:04.000 --> 02:09.000
This slide shows that the learned output is not limited to a single expression per emotion.

02:09.000 --> 02:15.000
For each row, the leftmost image is the neutral Furhat expression, and the remaining images are learned candidates.

02:16.000 --> 02:23.000
Even within the same emotion, such as happiness or sadness, the system discovers multiple visually different expressions.

02:24.000 --> 02:27.000
The same pattern appears for other emotions as well.

02:27.000 --> 02:33.000
The neutral expression on the left, our method discovers several different candidate expressions for fear and disgust.

02:33.000 --> 02:40.000
This shows that the learned expression pool captures diversity within the same emotion, instead of collapsing to one preset.

02:41.000 --> 02:44.000
We evaluated the learned expressions in an in-person user study.

02:44.000 --> 02:51.000
Participants interacted with the physical Furhat robot and judged whether the generated expressions conveyed the intended emotion.

02:51.000 --> 03:00.000
In the recognition task, participants watched the robot perform an expression, selected one of six emotions, and rated their confidence in that choice.

03:00.000 --> 03:07.000
This allowed us to test whether the expressions were readable to humans, not only whether they received high scores from the classifier.

03:07.000 --> 03:16.000
Participants also ranked expression candidates by intensity and naturalness, letting us evaluate the full expression pool beyond simple emotion recognition.

03:16.000 --> 03:22.000
Overall, the learned expressions were perceived as recognizable, diverse, natural, and emotionally intense.

